{"schema":1,"fm":{"schema":1,"id":"forecast-cycle-track","kind":"workflow","title":"Run a monthly forecast cycle","description":"Runs one monthly or quarterly forecast cycle from data checks and last cycle's accuracy, through a statistical baseline and logged adjustments, to an agreed forecast published with its uncertainty.","category":"forecasting","version":"1.0.0","status":"incubating","stage":["review","plan","ship"],"role":["data-analyst","operations-manager","financial-analyst","business-analyst"],"inputs":["dataset","text"],"output":["report","table","plan"],"risk":"read-only","invocation":"user","effort":"deep","interaction":"interactive","model_tier":"frontier","reasoning":"recommended","level":"intermediate","tags":["sales-and-operations-planning","consensus-forecast","forecast-process","reforecast"],"pairs_with":{"prompts":["measure-forecast-accuracy","forecast-time-series","add-event-adjustments-to-forecast","reconcile-top-down-and-bottom-up-forecasts","write-forecast-commentary","present-forecast-uncertainty"],"personas":["forecaster"]},"args":[{"name":"scope","description":"What is being forecast (metric, units, items or regions, level of detail), the horizon, the cycle (monthly or quarterly), and who uses the result for what decision.","type":"text","required":true},{"name":"data","description":"History of actuals, last cycle's forecast, any statistical and final versions, and known upcoming events. Describe files if they are large.","type":"text","required":true},{"name":"stakeholders","description":"Who contributes or signs off (sales, finance, operations, marketing), so the step that gathers input knows whom to ask.","type":"text"}],"steps":[{"id":"data-and-last-cycle","file":"steps/01-data-and-last-cycle.md","stage":"review","gate":"approve","artifact":"forecast-cycle/01-data-and-last-cycle.md"},{"id":"baseline","file":"steps/02-baseline.md","stage":"plan","gate":"approve","artifact":"forecast-cycle/02-baseline.md"},{"id":"adjustments","file":"steps/03-adjustments.md","stage":"plan","gate":"approve","artifact":"forecast-cycle/03-adjustments.md"},{"id":"agree","file":"steps/04-agree.md","stage":"review","gate":"approve","artifact":"forecast-cycle/04-agree.md"},{"id":"publish","file":"steps/05-publish.md","stage":"ship","gate":"none","artifact":"forecast-cycle/05-publish.md"}],"authorship":"ai-assisted","authors":["gabrielanhaia"],"last_reviewed":"2026-10-11","changelog":[{"version":"1.0.0","note":"First version."}]},"body":"Runs one forecast cycle the way a disciplined demand or finance planning team does: check the data and learn from last cycle's errors, build a statistical baseline, add only evidence-based adjustments with a log, agree one number across functions without hiding disagreements, and publish it with its uncertainty and what to watch. Each step writes one artifact and stops for approval; later steps reuse approved figures.\n\nScope:\n{{scope}}\n\n<data>\n{{data}}\n</data>\n{{#stakeholders}}\n\nStakeholders: {{stakeholders}}\n{{/stakeholders}}\n\nRules for every step:\n- Compute only from the data supplied or confirmed. Never invent actuals, accuracy figures or uplifts; when a number is an assumption, label it and say what would replace it.\n- Keep the forecast, the target and the plan separate. If a target is involved, show the gap; do not bend the forecast to meet it.\n- Every forecast figure carries a range.\n- Keep the baseline, each adjustment and the final number as separate columns so the cycle can be audited.\n- Keep a running list of open questions and owners, carried into step 5.","steps":[{"id":"data-and-last-cycle","file":"steps/01-data-and-last-cycle.md","stage":"review","gate":"approve","artifact":"forecast-cycle/01-data-and-last-cycle.md","text":"# Step 1: Data and last cycle\n\n1. Load the new actuals and check them: missing periods, late data, stock-outs or outages that censor demand, definition or hierarchy changes, one-off events.\n2. Score last cycle: compare the forecast made at the decision lag with the actuals using WAPE and bias (signed error), by item group. If statistical and final versions exist, compute forecast value added for the overrides.\n3. Note what last cycle got wrong and why (event, process, data, noise), and the lessons to apply this cycle.\n\nSections: Data checks, Last cycle accuracy, Bias, Value added by adjustments, Lessons, Open questions. Stop for approval."},{"id":"baseline","file":"steps/02-baseline.md","stage":"plan","gate":"approve","artifact":"forecast-cycle/02-baseline.md","text":"# Step 2: Statistical baseline\n\n1. Fit simple benchmarks (naive, seasonal naive) and one or two candidates (exponential smoothing, ARIMA, or Croston-type methods for intermittent items) on the approved history.\n2. Pick per series or group by a rolling-origin backtest at the horizon that matters; prefer the simpler method when the difference is small.\n3. Produce the baseline with 80% and 95% intervals by period, and the total at the level decisions are made.\n4. Flag series where the baseline is unreliable (short history, structural breaks) for extra attention in step 3.\n\nSections: Methods and backtest, Baseline forecast, Unreliable series, Open questions. Stop for approval."},{"id":"adjustments","file":"steps/03-adjustments.md","stage":"plan","gate":"approve","artifact":"forecast-cycle/03-adjustments.md","text":"# Step 3: Adjustments\n\n1. List the known events and inputs from stakeholders: promotions, price changes, launches, lost or won customers, capacity limits, moving holidays.\n2. For each, check whether the baseline already captures it; adjust only for what is new, sized from past analogues, including dips before and after promotions and cannibalisation.\n3. Push back on adjustments with no evidence or within normal error, and on any adjustment that is really a target.\n4. Write the adjustment log: event, periods, size, evidence, owner.\n\nSections: Inputs received, Already in baseline?, Adjustments applied, Adjustments declined and why, Adjusted forecast, Adjustment log. Stop for approval."},{"id":"agree","file":"steps/04-agree.md","stage":"review","gate":"approve","artifact":"forecast-cycle/04-agree.md","text":"# Step 4: Agree the number\n\n1. Line up the adjusted forecast with other views (finance top-down, sales bottom-up, the target) at the same level and units; show each gap.\n2. Explain the gaps by cause, reconcile the levels so they add up, and list the disagreements that remain as explicit decisions with owners.\n3. Translate the agreed forecast into planning numbers for the decisions it feeds (for example a higher percentile for stock or capacity when shortages cost more).\n\nSections: Views compared, Gaps and causes, Reconciled forecast, Decisions needed, Planning numbers. Stop for approval."},{"id":"publish","file":"steps/05-publish.md","stage":"ship","gate":"none","artifact":"forecast-cycle/05-publish.md","text":"# Step 5: Publish\n\n1. Write the forecast summary for the users: the agreed forecast with its range, the change from last cycle and why (a bridge), the main risks and opportunities with rough sizes, and the decisions needed.\n2. Recommend the chart (actuals plus a band showing the range) and plain wording for the uncertainty, such as \"about 1 month in 5 will fall outside this range\".\n3. List what to watch before the next cycle and the triggers for an early re-forecast.\n4. Carry forward open questions, owners and the adjustment log for scoring next cycle.\n\nSections: Forecast summary, Change from last cycle, Risks and opportunities, Chart and wording, What to watch, Carried forward."}]}
